00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.960
You could be running
an analytical workflow.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.870
You might be
archiving data as part

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.360
of your hybrid or
multi-cloud strategy,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.780
or maybe you're retiring
some legacy infrastructure,

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.280
in which case you need to move
the data off of it before you

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.190
do that retiring.

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.750
And to be able to do all
of these amazing things,

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:35.410
you need, often as a first
step, to move your data.

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.870
And what I often find, at
least in my conversations

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.608
with customers, is that
it's easy to look at this

00:01:40.608 --> 00:01:41.650
and say, oh, that's easy.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.840
That's the easiest part.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.190
We'll deal with that later.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:47.650
But often, it's much
harder than it looks.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.080
Sometimes you have
so much data--

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.100
petabytes and
petabytes of data--

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.660
the first problem is figuring
out what data you have,

00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:00.408
and which subset of that data
