00:03:16.908 --> 00:03:18.200
SCOTT FARRELL: Hello, everyone.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.160
My name is Scott, and I
work for Unity Technologies.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.320
I'm a DevOps engineer
on the Cloud team,

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.110
and we recently utilized
a storage transfer service

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:29.710
to move a large amount
of files from S3 to GCS.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:35.563
So what do we do?

00:03:35.563 --> 00:03:37.230
Unity is the creator
of the world's most

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.720
widely-used real-time
3D development platform.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.240
And what were we transferring?

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:48.500
500-plus terabytes of many files
varying in size from S3 to GCS.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:51.440
Unity was in the process
of migrating many service

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.640
infrastructure
components to GCP.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.010
So we had several
challenges and requirements,

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:58.540
and one of our
first requirements

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.510
was no downtime
for our customers.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:04.240
Another requirement was
ensuring that all of our files

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:05.980
made it to the end bucket.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:07.040
We had some files--

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.570
actually, many files-- that had
an older legacy naming pattern,

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:12.100
so we had to take
extra precaution

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.040
to make sure all
of them arrived.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.459
So some of the requirements-- or
actually, the challenges we had

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:20.260
were updating our
service code prior

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:23.230
to handle this
no-downtime scenario.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:24.850
We also couldn't
use the web console

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:26.350
for this because
of the complexity

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:27.670
and the number of files.

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:30.507
So we utilized the
STS API directly,

00:04:30.507 --> 00:04:32.590
which helped us keep track
of the actual transfers

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.491
as they were happening.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.510
So how did we
organize as a team?

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:42.310
First of all,
another team in Unity

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.000
had already done a
transfer project.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.580
Theirs was slightly
less complicated

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.770
because they had
naming conventions

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.330
that were consistent.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:52.030
So we essentially took some
of the code that they had,

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:56.290
some wrapper script, and
tailored them to our needs.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:59.940
So our devs did a service code
prep to deal with where to read

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.290
and where to write files
during this process.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:05.780
And so DevOps prepared scripts
to test these transfers

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:07.640
from our dev and
stage environments

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.190
before the final production run.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:14.380
So once the developers
deployed the code,

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.070
we did the storage
transfers from dev in stage,

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:21.568
and we did things like
transfer speed times,

00:05:21.568 --> 00:05:23.110
logging any errors
recorded, and test

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:25.240
runs to pave way for
final production use.

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:29.970
So some of the
tactics we used that

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.830
led up to this transfer event--

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.140
speed tests were performed
for transferring the files.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:37.290
We did it in both regional
and multi-regional buckets.

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:40.390
The end bucket was
multi-regional for us.

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:43.020
So we also tested a few
transfers with smaller data

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.760
in buckets that were
in the same regions

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:48.450
or close to the S3
and GCS regions.

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:51.030
The final process
was for regional.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:54.180
Actually, we transferred from
regional to regional and then

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:56.535
inside of GCS, we did
multi-regional transfer.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:03.650
So some of our key takeaways
and best practices--

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:05.390
these transfers
can be complicated.

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:07.820
So devs and DevOps
worked together

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:10.580
to break down the process.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:12.830
Also, I suggest
if your team can,

00:06:12.830 --> 00:06:15.470
find any internal experts that
have done this transfer before

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:18.050
because you can learn from
what they've gone through

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:22.841
and maybe adapt your
transfer from their transfer.

00:06:22.841 --> 00:06:25.040
So how to prep for the transfer?

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.882
Do lots of testing.

00:06:26.882 --> 00:06:28.340
So testing will
help you understand

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:31.420
what to do in case you have
problems in the production run.

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.560
It also gives you a good
benchmark for performance.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.140
It also may impact
your production code,

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.770
so know what your workload is.

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:42.080
Also, consider the
cost of transfer.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.280
We transferred over
public internet from S3.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.330
So you have egress costs.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:49.380
Some best practices.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.360
Evaluate all your
transfer options.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.990
Also, consider temporary or
regional buckets for your sync

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.640
before you go multi-regional.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.370
So if you have to sync again
you can have time to sync

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:01.650
to your single regional
bucket and then decide

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.430
what to do from there.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:05.190
Have a really good plan.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:08.640
Another final suggestion is
have service accounts ready

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:09.765
with STS roles.

00:07:09.765 --> 00:07:11.890
You're limited to a certain
number of jobs per day,

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:13.980
so if you run out of
jobs, you can always

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:15.563
use one of your other
service accounts

00:07:15.563 --> 00:07:16.980
that are ready for the process.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:21.107
And most of all, prepare
for any issues or mistakes.

