00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:37.040
So one of the questions
that'll come up

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.172
as part of your
transfer planning--

00:07:39.172 --> 00:07:40.880
or a very common
question that comes up--

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:44.150
is, how long is my
transfer going to take?

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:47.120
Now there's a big component of
that, which is what Scott just

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:47.930
talked about.

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:51.110
All of the process,
organizational work, testing,

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:54.470
and prep leading up to
the transfer event, that's

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:58.400
a big portion of the time for
a transfer project to complete.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.483
But once you've
gone beyond that--

00:08:01.483 --> 00:08:03.650
if you're talking just
about, I have some data here.

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:05.942
I have some bytes, and I need
to move them over there--

00:08:05.942 --> 00:08:07.910
typically there
are two big factors

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:09.890
that drive transfer times.

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:12.850
The first is how much data
are you going to move,

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:16.660
and what bandwidth do
you have to move it?

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:18.660
So just to get a sense
from people in the room--

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:23.040
and I'll try my best to see
your hands with this light--

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.860
how many people in this
room have a connection

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.010
to the public internet
or to Google Cloud

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:34.090
directly that's more than
100 megabits per second?

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:37.220
OK, a few hands are up.

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:39.010
How many of you
have a connection

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:42.929
that is faster than one
gigabits per second?

00:08:42.929 --> 00:08:46.110
OK, just one or two people.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:47.170
OK, one last question.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:50.760
How many of you have no idea
how fast your connection is

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.280
to GCP or the public internet?

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:54.730
OK, a lot of you.

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:58.068
So for those of you
that are in that bucket,

00:08:58.068 --> 00:08:59.610
you already know
one of the questions

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.650
that you need to start asking.

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:04.020
Now, based on the answers
to these questions,

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.210
you can get a pretty wide
spectrum of outcomes.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:09.300
Your transfers can take
anywhere from minutes or hours

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.590
to many days or even weeks.

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:13.520
So it's important to
understand this upfront,

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:18.340
and it helps you clarify what
options you should consider.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:20.970
Now, another way
to think about this

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:24.690
is to think about how
"close," quote, unquote,

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:27.940
or far your data is
based on your bandwidth.

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:30.510
So on the very left-hand
side of this spectrum

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:34.800
here is data that you could
consider close to you.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:36.450
So say, for instance,
you're moving

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:40.320
data within Google Cloud,
between two Google Cloud data

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:41.280
centers.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.890
That's going to go over a
very high-bandwidth pipe

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:45.510
that Google has provisioned.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.940
And so even if you're moving
a large amount of data,

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:49.847
it's going to show
up pretty fast.

00:09:49.847 --> 00:09:51.930
In that sense, that data's
is pretty close to you.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.150
Whenever you need
it, you can get it.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:55.980
Whereas on the other
end of the spectrum,

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:59.490
let's say you have a data
center outpost on a mountaintop.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:02.460
It has a satellite link
that's really slow,

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:04.940
or maybe it has no connection
to the internet at all,

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:06.690
in which case, even a
small amount of data

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:08.980
will take a long
time to get to you.

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:13.387
And so that is a way to think
about data that's far away.

00:10:13.387 --> 00:10:14.970
So this is the
spectrum on which we're

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:18.270
thinking about talking
through the options that

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:20.640
are available for moving
data in Google today.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.290
So we'll start with
the leftmost, which

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:25.080
is data that's close to you
with the Cloud Storage Transfer

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.185
Service.

00:10:27.185 --> 00:10:28.560
Now, this product
is specifically

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:32.670
optimized to move data from
other clouds into GCP--

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:37.420
for instance, if you're
moving data from S3 into GCS.

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:39.680
And it's used for a lot
of different purposes.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:42.410
Customers sometimes use
it for archival and backup

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.550
as part of their
multi-cloud strategy.

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:47.490
Sometimes they'll use it as
part of an analytical workload.

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:50.400
They'll load data
from S3 into GCS,

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.010
and then put it
into BigQuery or run

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.530
some of our machine learning
products against it.

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:58.740
Or they might be just
moving data within GCS,

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:03.220
moving between a GCS bucket
in one region to another.

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:05.790
Now today, this product
supports pulling data

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:09.540
from S3, GCS, and any
web-addressable HTTP data

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:11.130
source.

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:14.460
It can move data just one
time, or you can set it up

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:18.210
to run transfers every day,
like every day at 6 o'clock,

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:20.340
move my data over, please.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:24.847
And it's supported
wherever GCS works today.

00:11:24.847 --> 00:11:27.180
So what I'll go through now
is just a quick walk-through

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:28.800
of how this product works.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.050
It's a pretty simple product.

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:35.805
First, to find it, you go into
Cloud Console our web GUI.

00:11:35.805 --> 00:11:37.680
And you go into Storage,
and underneath there

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:39.963
will be an option
called Transfer.

00:11:39.963 --> 00:11:41.880
If you click on that,
you will have the option

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:43.470
to create a transfer.

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:44.748
Pretty straightforward.

00:11:44.748 --> 00:11:46.290
And when you create
a transfer, we're

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:48.760
going to ask you
for three things.

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:52.728
What data do you want to move,
where do you want to move it,

00:11:52.728 --> 00:11:54.061
and when do you want to move it?

00:11:54.061 --> 00:11:56.320
That's it.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:58.820
So the first thing, what
data do you want to move?

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:02.100
So in this example, we're
moving data from an S3 bucket,

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:05.580
so we'll ask you for your bucket
name, your access credentials--

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:07.440
so we can read the data.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.360
And then we also have
some optional filters

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:10.080
you can give us.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:13.860
So if, for instance, you wanted
to move only the files that

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:17.010
were modified last week, we can
filter that out and only move

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:19.880
that data for you.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.060
The second step is
to tell us, where

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:24.188
do you want to move
this data, which is

00:12:24.188 --> 00:12:25.480
very similar to the first step.

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:28.465
Just tell us what GCS bucket
you want to move the data into.

00:12:28.465 --> 00:12:30.347
As simple as that.

00:12:30.347 --> 00:12:32.930
And then, finally, you tell us
when you want to move the data.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:36.610
You can start moving the data
right now once you hit Done,

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:38.320
or you can schedule
it to run nightly,

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:40.330
as I mentioned before,
if you want to have

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:43.108
this be a continuous process.

00:12:43.108 --> 00:12:44.150
So it's just that simple.

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:46.000
And once you kick
this off, there

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:50.880
is a reporting GUI that
will help you track,

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:52.382
OK, where all your transfers?

00:12:52.382 --> 00:12:53.590
How much data has been moved?

00:12:53.590 --> 00:12:55.390
How much data is left?

00:12:55.390 --> 00:12:57.640
If there are any errors, you
can go look up the errors

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.310
and resolve them.

00:12:59.310 --> 00:13:00.470
And it's just that simple.

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:01.810
No provisioning to do.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:02.500
No other setup.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:05.110
Just give us your credentials,
tell us these three things,

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:06.068
and you're ready to go.

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:11.140
Now, the next step
on the spectrum

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:13.990
is if, let's say, your data
is a little bit further away.

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:16.600
So perhaps it's
a networking link

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.380
that's provisioned by your colo
or you provisioned yourself

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:22.780
at your corporate
office data center,

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:24.880
so it might be a
little bit slower.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:29.020
For that, we have a
product called gsutil.

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:34.000
gsutil is designed to move
data from your data center

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:37.000
that you operate
into Google Cloud.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:38.560
It's a command
line utility, which

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:40.630
means you can use it
in an ad-hoc basis

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:43.780
in your day-to-day workflows,
just invoke it from a shell,

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:46.315
or you can put it in a
script as part of a cron job.

00:13:46.315 --> 00:13:49.660
It works like any other
command line utility.

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:51.430
You can also run it
in a continuous mode.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:52.930
So you can run it
in the background.

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:55.050
It will sit and
listen on a directory.

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:58.020
And whenever new data comes
in or data is updated,

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:01.810
it will push that
data to a GCS bucket.

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:04.090
Now, in addition to
these two modes--

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.355
continuous sync and copy--
it also supports streaming.

00:14:06.355 --> 00:14:08.230
So if you have a Unix
command you're running,

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:11.290
you can pipe it to gsutil,
and it'll stream the output

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:12.970
to a GCS object.

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:16.780
Underneath the covers, it also
supports automatic retries,

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:19.846
encryption, and GCS
object versioning.

00:14:19.846 --> 00:14:23.390
Now, gsutil has
a lot of options.

00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:25.480
I'm going to go
through a few that I've

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:30.260
found in my experience customers
find to be pretty useful.

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:33.310
So let's just walk
through those now.

00:14:33.310 --> 00:14:36.680
So the first is, how do
I get this to go fast?

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:40.880
One option I always recommend is
to use the -m flag for gsutil.

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:44.480
What this triggers is that
it runs the gsutil process

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:47.300
in a multi-threaded mode
so that many, many threads

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:51.230
inside the gsutil process are
pushing many files in parallel

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:53.780
to the cloud.

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:57.420
You almost always
want to use this.

00:14:57.420 --> 00:14:59.350
Now, let's say
that's not enough.

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:00.370
Let's say you have--

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:04.332
running gsutil, and it's
using up three gigabits

00:15:04.332 --> 00:15:05.540
per second of your bandwidth.

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:06.850
That's pretty good.

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:09.540
But you have a
10-gigabits-per-second link.

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:12.100
So how do I use up my bandwidth?

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:14.850
So what we recommend
there is to run gsutil

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:18.450
across multiple hosts
or multiple processes.

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:21.270
What you would do is
partition your data upfront,

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:25.170
and then you'd kick off multiple
gsutil command line invocations

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:27.220
to run the data
transfer in parallel.

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:32.850
So for instance, I run
gsutil on subdirectory one.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:35.790
Then I run gsutil
on subdirectory two.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:38.040
And then I run gsutil
on subdirectory three

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:39.710
on machine number two.

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:41.950
And all those gsutils
are run in parallel,

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:44.640
and that way, you can
fill up your pipe.

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:45.925
Now, let's say you do this.

00:15:45.925 --> 00:15:47.800
You're still going to
have one other problem.

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:52.523
What if I have a really big
file, a 100-gigabyte file?

00:15:52.523 --> 00:15:54.690
Well, it's going to get
mapped to one thread and one

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:56.370
of these gsutil
processes, and I got

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:58.578
to just sit there and wait
for that thread to finish.

00:15:58.578 --> 00:16:00.040
That's going to take forever.

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:02.850
So for that scenario,
we have a feature called

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:04.170
parallel composite uploads.

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:06.900
What it'll do is,
for large files,

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:08.670
it'll break those
files up into chunks

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:10.560
and transfer the
chunks in parallel

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:13.740
so you don't get bottlenecked
on large files in your transfer.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:20.240
Now I'll just go through a few
other assorted tips and tricks

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:22.020
with this tool.

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:23.940
The first is
controlling bandwidth.

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:26.990
So in some situations,
some customers will say,

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:30.538
I want to be able to limit
how much bandwidth gsutil uses

00:16:30.538 --> 00:16:32.330
so that I can preserve
some of my bandwidth

00:16:32.330 --> 00:16:37.102
for my existing workloads
in my data center.

00:16:37.102 --> 00:16:39.710
And unfortunately, gsutil
still has no native support,

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:42.380
but you can use command
line utilities like trickle

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:46.690
to control how much
bandwidth gsutil is using.

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:47.930
Second common ask is--

00:16:47.930 --> 00:16:49.640
especially if you're
doing archival-- is,

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:51.010
I want to say my file metadata.

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:52.290
How do I do that?

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:54.560
So you can use -P
option on gsutil,

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:57.530
which will preserve all
of your POSIX metadata

00:16:57.530 --> 00:16:59.540
into GCS object metadata.

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:01.590
So it will preserve
that for you.

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:03.260
And then finally,
for people that

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:08.240
are worried about accidental
overwrites, you can use -n.

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:11.990
And that'll ensure that if
a GCS object already exists,

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:13.829
gsutil will not overwrite it.

00:17:13.829 --> 00:17:16.454
So if that's a concern, if
someone fat-fingers something

00:17:16.454 --> 00:17:18.079
or you just want to
make sure you never

00:17:18.079 --> 00:17:20.780
lose data, that's something
you should look into.

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:23.630
Now, as I said before,
gsutil has a lot of options.

00:17:23.630 --> 00:17:25.970
This is just a very
small subset of them.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:27.599
If you're curious
to learn more, I

00:17:27.599 --> 00:17:30.620
suggest you go and look at
our online web documentation

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:34.130
on gsutil, which is a pretty
comprehensive listing of all

00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:36.380
the things that gsutil can do.

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:38.690
And with that, let me hand
it off to my colleague, Ben,

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:42.574
who will talk about some
other transfer options.

00:17:42.574 --> 00:17:43.632
Thanks.

00:17:43.632 --> 00:17:44.590
BEN CHONG: Thanks, Ash.

00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:49.050
All right.

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:49.900
So that's me.

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:53.430
I'm another product manager
on the Google Transfer team.

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:53.930
All right.

00:17:53.930 --> 00:17:56.360
So we have talked about
cloud transfer service

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:58.050
for cloud-to-cloud transfers.

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:02.035
We have talked about gsutil
for on-prem-to-cloud transfers

00:18:02.035 --> 00:18:05.480
where you have a reasonable
network connectivity.

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:08.390
What about locations where
you don't have network

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:11.550
connectivity or not enough?

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:13.910
We have a lot of
customers who do

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:17.348
e-commerce, who serve content
from their data centers.

00:18:17.348 --> 00:18:18.890
So you would think
that a lot of them

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:21.970
would have enough bandwidth
to upload terabytes of data

00:18:21.970 --> 00:18:22.820
to cloud.

00:18:22.820 --> 00:18:25.760
The reality is that they
use all that bandwidth

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:27.050
for production use--

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:29.447
to serve content,
to run e-commerce.

00:18:29.447 --> 00:18:31.280
You don't want to suck
up all that bandwidth

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:33.380
to upload a few hundred
terabytes of data

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:35.960
to cloud and bring down your
whole system and your business

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:38.160
as well.

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:42.590
And the other option is,
well, why can't we just

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:43.970
provision more bandwidth?

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:49.580
It's not as easy as that
because of cost, of time.

00:18:49.580 --> 00:18:51.740
You have to sign maybe
a two-year agreement

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:53.690
with your ISP.

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:55.700
And if you are just doing
a one-time transfer,

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:58.020
it's probably not worth it.

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:00.770
So that's where we talk about
the offline transfer use

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:04.010
case for situations where
you have very little network

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:07.250
bandwidth or you have no
bandwidth at all-- for example,

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:10.490
you have a data center up
in a mountain somewhere.

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:14.630
And Google's product is
called Transfer Appliance.

00:19:14.630 --> 00:19:17.990
So the picture of the
appliance that you see here

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:20.090
is a 2U storage server.

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:22.830
It can be racked in
your data center.

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:27.320
It has a usable capacity
of about 80 terabytes.

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:30.890
And this is really inexpensive.

00:19:30.890 --> 00:19:32.840
We ship it to you overnight.

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:35.570
You rack it, load data to
it, ship it back to us,

00:19:35.570 --> 00:19:37.070
and we'll upload
the data for you.

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:42.710
Before I go into use
cases, the picture here

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:46.160
is of our bigger appliance.

00:19:46.160 --> 00:19:52.040
It's a 4U form factor, usable
capacity about 400 terabytes.

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:55.100
This is a pretty big box,
so most of our customers

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:59.070
will have this sitting on
their data center floor.

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:01.220
So let's talk about use cases.

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:04.820
Number one use case here,
which we encounter very often,

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:08.360
is where customers
really like the idea

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:10.820
of running their workloads in
cloud, machine learning, data

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:13.320
analytics, and so
on and so forth.

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:15.710
But then they run into a
chicken-and-egg problem.

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:18.000
You have all that data.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:19.830
You cannot convince
your CIO, CEO,

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:22.940
to fund larger network pipes.

00:20:22.940 --> 00:20:24.710
So how do you get all
that data to cloud

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:28.530
to prove that cloud has
value for your company?

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:32.150
So we have customers who order
an appliance, just a small one,

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:37.010
TA100, ship us 70
terabytes of data,

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:40.560
run ML, run some data
analytics, say, hey, it works.

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.930
There's real value
for the business.

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:46.100
And that's where they get
agreement from management

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:48.910
to invest in more
network bandwidth

00:20:48.910 --> 00:20:50.840
and so on and so forth.

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:53.330
Other use cases, including
seeding backups to Cloud,

00:20:53.330 --> 00:20:54.995
and we'll talk
about that in a bit.

00:20:54.995 --> 00:20:58.560
Data center migration,
large archival moves.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:01.980
So where you are shutting
down a data center,

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:04.340
you may want to move
all that data to cloud

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:11.010
before you actually shut it down
and obsolete your old service.

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:11.510
All right.

00:21:11.510 --> 00:21:13.450
So how do you get one?

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:15.070
The Transfer Appliance
service is now

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:17.440
available in the US and Canada.

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:19.900
It's also available in the EU.

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:21.700
You basically
order the appliance

00:21:21.700 --> 00:21:26.918
online on Cloud Console
under the Storage section,

00:21:26.918 --> 00:21:28.460
and you basically
fill up this thing.

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:32.080
This is how you fill it up.

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:34.150
There's a contact
email that's optional,

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:37.750
but how we work this
service is that it's not

00:21:37.750 --> 00:21:38.980
automated at the back.

00:21:38.980 --> 00:21:41.860
So what I like to say is
that there's a human being

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:43.330
behind the service.

00:21:43.330 --> 00:21:47.500
So that's a nice thing because,
Ash was saying earlier, moving

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:50.795
a lot of data is not easy.

00:21:50.795 --> 00:21:52.420
We don't want to ship
you the appliance

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:54.557
and like, go figure it out.

00:21:54.557 --> 00:21:56.890
So we would like to work with
our customers hand-in-hand

00:21:56.890 --> 00:21:59.311
to make sure that your
data moves are successful.

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:05.190
Again, the onboarding
process is very easy.

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:06.420
You request an appliance.

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:07.560
We ship it to you.

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:09.930
You fill it up,
ship it back to us.

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:13.500
We will upload the data
for you, and then you

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:16.500
figure out where you
want to put it in Cloud.

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:17.220
One note.

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:20.061
How many Canadians
are here in the room?

00:22:20.061 --> 00:22:21.390
No, I don't see anybody.

00:22:21.390 --> 00:22:21.890
One.

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:22.940
OK.

00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:26.240
So how this works for Canada
is that the data upload

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:27.650
will be based in the US.

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:31.057
We haven't set up a
center in Canada yet.

00:22:31.057 --> 00:22:32.390
So we'll ship you the appliance.

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:33.830
You ship it back
to us in the US.

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:35.480
We'll upload the data.

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:39.550
And in case you're worried about
security, the next two slides

00:22:39.550 --> 00:22:40.980
from now, we'll talk about that.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:41.480
OK.

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:44.790
So setup and configuration
is pretty straightforward.

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:47.180
This is a standard
server like those of you

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:50.660
are familiar with the
IT hardware, the picture

00:22:50.660 --> 00:22:53.870
that you see here is the
back of the big server

00:22:53.870 --> 00:22:58.190
standard power connectors,
standard copper ethernet

00:22:58.190 --> 00:22:58.820
connectors.

00:22:58.820 --> 00:23:01.100
And we also support fiber optic.

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:05.330
We do provide the
SFP+ transceivers.

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:08.720
There's some bandwidth
incompatibilities

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:10.220
across different
standards, so we'll

00:23:10.220 --> 00:23:12.590
work with you to figure
out whether what we provide

00:23:12.590 --> 00:23:15.300
is sufficient for your network.

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:17.590
And we also support
network aggregation.

00:23:17.590 --> 00:23:20.060
So you see here there
are four network ports,

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:22.880
so we can run as fast
as 40 gigs per second

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:25.330
so network is not
in a bottleneck.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:27.530
And talking about
bottlenecks, a lot

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:29.390
of times when we
work with customers

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:33.372
who have lots of
very small files,

00:23:33.372 --> 00:23:38.870
they complain that copying files
from the existing file servers

00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:41.320
[INAUDIBLE] are too slow.

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:44.977
It is mainly because of
the billions of small files

00:23:44.977 --> 00:23:46.310
that they're trying to pull out.

00:23:46.310 --> 00:23:48.110
This is something
that customers will

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:53.433
have to deal with, kind of like
reading data from the files.

00:23:53.433 --> 00:23:54.850
Obviously, once
that data has been

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:57.310
[INAUDIBLE] from the
original file systems,

00:23:57.310 --> 00:23:59.790
everything else is pretty quick.

00:23:59.790 --> 00:24:00.290
OK.

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:02.810
So talking about
security, the key point

00:24:02.810 --> 00:24:07.710
here is data is always
encrypted at rest, in transit.

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:10.130
We, Google, don't need the
keys to upload your data.

00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:12.950
So customers keep and
control your keys.

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:16.130
The data is encrypted when
it's copied onto the appliance,

00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:20.210
and customers will decrypt
the data in their GCP project

00:24:20.210 --> 00:24:23.240
once the data has been uploaded.

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:25.220
And then what happens
with appliance

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:28.087
after you ship it back to
us and after the data has

00:24:28.087 --> 00:24:28.670
been uploaded?

00:24:28.670 --> 00:24:31.040
We do a secure wipe
of all the drives.

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:33.730
We reinstall the
OS and applications

00:24:33.730 --> 00:24:36.230
for the next customer, so it's
like getting a new appliance.

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:41.920
So a quick security summary.

00:24:41.920 --> 00:24:45.420
So you, the customer,
owns the encryption keys.

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:47.010
You manage that.

00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:49.357
Data is always
encrypted in transit.

00:24:49.357 --> 00:24:51.690
We upload the encrypted data
into what we call a staging

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:55.320
bucket, and then you decrypt
that within your own GCP

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:56.130
project.

00:24:56.130 --> 00:24:58.390
And then we wipe the appliances.

00:24:58.390 --> 00:25:01.422
Wipe certificates are
available, obviously,

00:25:01.422 --> 00:25:02.380
for a lot of customers.

00:25:02.380 --> 00:25:06.270
They find that it's very
important for audit purposes.

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:08.100
With that, I would
like to hand you off

00:25:08.100 --> 00:25:11.550
to Deepak, who will talk
about one of the use cases

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:13.034
for Transfer Appliance.

00:25:17.252 --> 00:25:17.960
DEEPAK VERMA: Hi.

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:18.960
My name is Deepak Verma.

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:21.020
I'm director of product
management at Commvault,

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:25.160
and we're one of the vendors
that support Transfer Appliance

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:26.520
on-prem.

00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:30.030
So I'll start at
10,000-foot level.

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:32.240
This is our blueprint
architecture

00:25:32.240 --> 00:25:35.450
of what we can perform
with Google Cloud.

00:25:35.450 --> 00:25:37.730
So break down the architecture.

00:25:37.730 --> 00:25:39.470
On the left side,
we've got a lot

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:43.910
of customers that are running
various type of workloads.

00:25:43.910 --> 00:25:46.530
It could be VM workflows
databases, applications,

00:25:46.530 --> 00:25:49.693
not just flat files that
may need to get transferred.

00:25:49.693 --> 00:25:51.110
You've got a
Commvault component--

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:52.970
we call it the VSA--

00:25:52.970 --> 00:25:56.060
that's capable of transferring
or writing that data into a GCS

00:25:56.060 --> 00:25:59.030
bucket directly.

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:02.868
Transfer Appliance is something
that we supported recently

00:26:02.868 --> 00:26:04.910
as a mechanism to transfer
large amounts of data,

00:26:04.910 --> 00:26:08.390
and I'll get into one
versus the other one.

00:26:08.390 --> 00:26:11.330
Once you're in GCP, you've
got a number of options

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:13.830
that you can use.

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:16.970
First off, you can obviously
protect GCU workloads,

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:18.920
virtual machines,
and applications

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:21.890
within those
workloads just as you

00:26:21.890 --> 00:26:24.890
would on an on-prem environment
and write it to a GCS bucket.

00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:30.170
Other components of GCP that
we support are Filestore.

00:26:30.170 --> 00:26:33.170
So you can back that up as
though it were an on-prem NAS

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:35.990
and write that data to GCS.

00:26:35.990 --> 00:26:39.140
Within GCS, we support tiering
just like you would in on-prem.

00:26:39.140 --> 00:26:41.490
You would write to a
standard tier data.

00:26:41.490 --> 00:26:44.250
And then maybe after
a period of time,

00:26:44.250 --> 00:26:47.000
you want to move that to cheaper
storage, to deeper archive,

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:48.468
you can move that
into a cold line.

00:26:48.468 --> 00:26:50.010
So you've got
flexibility to do that.

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:51.718
And one of the things
that I put on there

00:26:51.718 --> 00:26:53.120
is actually protection to-from.

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:56.570
So not only can you
read the data from GCS,

00:26:56.570 --> 00:26:58.160
but you can also write to GCS.

00:26:58.160 --> 00:26:59.780
In other words, GCS protection.

00:26:59.780 --> 00:27:02.510
So I want to double-click on
the Transfer Appliance component

00:27:02.510 --> 00:27:06.970
and talk through use cases
that customers have brought up

00:27:06.970 --> 00:27:07.470
to us.

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:11.610
So the first one is
migration of existing data.

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:13.760
So what this means
is there may be

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:15.200
hundreds of terabytes
or petabytes

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:17.330
of data that may
be sitting on-prem.

00:27:17.330 --> 00:27:19.610
Typically where we've
seen it is elimination

00:27:19.610 --> 00:27:21.820
of those long-term silos.

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:23.570
Some industries have
seven-year retention.

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:25.460
Others have up to
30-year retention

00:27:25.460 --> 00:27:27.030
that they must maintain data.

00:27:27.030 --> 00:27:29.753
And today, it's sitting
in tape libraries.

00:27:29.753 --> 00:27:31.670
It could be sitting in
virtual tape libraries.

00:27:31.670 --> 00:27:33.378
It could be sitting
in dedupe appliances,

00:27:33.378 --> 00:27:35.360
and they want to
move that to Cloud.

00:27:35.360 --> 00:27:37.560
So going back to
Ash's comment earlier,

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:39.740
it just doesn't make
sense to put hundreds

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:41.540
of gigabytes of
network connectivity

00:27:41.540 --> 00:27:42.860
to get that transfer done.

00:27:42.860 --> 00:27:45.853
So this is where
Appliance comes in handy.

00:27:45.853 --> 00:27:47.270
So use case there
ends up becoming

00:27:47.270 --> 00:27:50.090
what we call an auxiliary
copy, which is basically

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:53.300
making a copy of
that data, putting it

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:57.050
onto a Transfer Appliance, and
then bringing it up into GCS.

00:27:57.050 --> 00:27:58.490
The second use case is seeding.

00:27:58.490 --> 00:28:01.520
So maybe it's a net
new project where

00:28:01.520 --> 00:28:03.820
they want to forego old
data, so we're going

00:28:03.820 --> 00:28:06.470
to start backing up to GCS.

00:28:06.470 --> 00:28:10.170
However, for the amount
of data that we have,

00:28:10.170 --> 00:28:12.160
there's no option
to perform a full.

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:14.800
So I'll get to an example
here pretty shortly,

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:17.180
but the full would take a
considerable amount of time.

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:19.250
And you don't want to
size your bandwidth

00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:21.110
for that full capacity
because you're not

00:28:21.110 --> 00:28:23.640
going to be doing full
backups on a daily basis.

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:27.530
So we call this a full
or a baseline backup.

00:28:27.530 --> 00:28:29.320
The third use case is
pretty interesting--

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:31.050
it's popped up more--

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:31.820
is air-gapped.

00:28:31.820 --> 00:28:33.950
And this is more for
ransomware protection

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:37.490
where you truly want a copy
of data that is not tied

00:28:37.490 --> 00:28:39.210
into your production network.

00:28:39.210 --> 00:28:41.210
So there are cases where
customers have actually

00:28:41.210 --> 00:28:43.310
put their data into
a GCS bucket knowing

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:46.520
that they can recover
in Cloud if they ever

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:48.350
hit that eventuality.

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:52.250
The benefit that you get
from a backup product,

00:28:52.250 --> 00:28:53.690
driving that is--

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:55.940
obviously you're overcoming
bandwidth limitations

00:28:55.940 --> 00:28:58.160
with integration of
Transfer Appliance,

00:28:58.160 --> 00:29:02.690
but you get the benefit of the
deduplication and compression

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:05.490
as opposed to just copying
the data flat, if you will.

00:29:05.490 --> 00:29:07.320
You have the ability
to perform encryption.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:09.650
So Ben mentioned encryption.

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:11.777
However, you can still do
encryption at that level.

00:29:11.777 --> 00:29:13.610
So if your backup data
is already encrypted,

00:29:13.610 --> 00:29:15.823
that encryption
transfers through.

00:29:15.823 --> 00:29:18.365
And the other benefit-- if you
remember the architecture from

00:29:18.365 --> 00:29:19.430
the previous slide--

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:22.460
you don't need any additional
appliances or gateways

00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:25.130
in order to actually move the
data to Transfer Appliance.

00:29:25.130 --> 00:29:27.420
So it becomes a seamless.

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:30.020
So we treat, from an
architecture standpoint,

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:34.010
Transfer Appliance as though
it were an on-prem GCS bucket.

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:36.500
And where that comes in
handy is that once the data

00:29:36.500 --> 00:29:39.140
has been moved to
GCS, then you don't

00:29:39.140 --> 00:29:42.630
have to worry about having to
change formats on that one.

00:29:42.630 --> 00:29:48.440
So adding a few components
to Ben's steps here--

00:29:48.440 --> 00:29:51.260
so I've changed it from
four steps to eight steps--

00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:54.600
you perform the ordering,
you do the setup, et cetera.

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:57.260
And then there's a component
of what to transfer.

00:29:57.260 --> 00:29:59.390
So within the Commvault
infrastructure,

00:29:59.390 --> 00:30:00.770
you can actually
run reports that

00:30:00.770 --> 00:30:03.860
will tell you about the data
itself, when it's been touched,

00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:05.000
what files it is.

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:07.677
So you can really select
what specifically you

00:30:07.677 --> 00:30:08.720
want to transfer here.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:12.410
Typically, what customers have
gone for is they'll select,

00:30:12.410 --> 00:30:13.820
I mean, anything beyond 30 days.

00:30:13.820 --> 00:30:15.710
So they'll consider
zero to 30 day

00:30:15.710 --> 00:30:18.320
or zero to 14 days
operational recovery, things

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:20.392
that they need on-prem
to recover immediately.

00:30:20.392 --> 00:30:22.100
But then beyond that,
they're maintaining

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:24.020
long-term retention,
and they want

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:25.310
to transfer that type of data.

00:30:25.310 --> 00:30:27.140
Or they're going after
large static files,

00:30:27.140 --> 00:30:29.473
so you can run
reports against that.

00:30:29.473 --> 00:30:30.890
When you transfer
the data, you've

00:30:30.890 --> 00:30:32.870
got two options, as
I mentioned earlier.

00:30:32.870 --> 00:30:35.510
You've got the ability to do an
auxiliary copy, which basically

00:30:35.510 --> 00:30:37.830
means it's sitting in a
backup format somewhere--

00:30:37.830 --> 00:30:39.950
VTL, tape library, et cetera--

00:30:39.950 --> 00:30:42.500
and you want to make
a copy of that copy.

00:30:42.500 --> 00:30:45.140
Or it's a full backup
that you're baselining.

00:30:45.140 --> 00:30:48.770
The next step ends up being
disabling any further writes.

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:51.590
So once the Transfer
Appliance is full,

00:30:51.590 --> 00:30:54.390
we want to completely disable
any writes and data aging.

00:30:54.390 --> 00:30:54.890
Why?

00:30:54.890 --> 00:30:56.390
Because it's going
to take some time

00:30:56.390 --> 00:30:58.710
to transfer the actual
physical appliance, to ship it,

00:30:58.710 --> 00:31:00.170
to stage the data out.

00:31:00.170 --> 00:31:03.290
And in that meantime, if
you've got policies setup that

00:31:03.290 --> 00:31:07.400
may want to expire data based
on your SLAs or your business

00:31:07.400 --> 00:31:10.380
requirements, it's not going to
be able to find that appliance.

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:13.110
So that's something
to keep in mind.

00:31:13.110 --> 00:31:16.200
The other aspect is, what
happens to daily transfers?

00:31:16.200 --> 00:31:19.400
And one of the things that
we've found is that it's very

00:31:19.400 --> 00:31:21.470
beneficial to have
the daily transfers--

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:24.710
especially as GCS it's going
to be your ultimate target--

00:31:24.710 --> 00:31:29.500
to actually have those sent
to GCS as incrementals.

00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:33.503
And I'll get to a little
bit more around that one.

00:31:33.503 --> 00:31:34.670
Then you ship the appliance.

00:31:34.670 --> 00:31:39.110
You rehydrate the data,
as Ben described earlier.

00:31:39.110 --> 00:31:42.140
From there, once
the data actually

00:31:42.140 --> 00:31:45.998
ends up in your GCS
bucket within GCP,

00:31:45.998 --> 00:31:48.040
all you have to do within
the Commvault interface

00:31:48.040 --> 00:31:50.540
is actually flip a bit to
tell it that now you're

00:31:50.540 --> 00:31:52.610
writing in a Cloud
library as opposed

00:31:52.610 --> 00:31:57.060
to a pseudo-Cloud library that
was emulated on the Transfer

00:31:57.060 --> 00:31:57.560
Appliance.

00:31:57.560 --> 00:31:59.000
And why this is important
is because we're

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:00.920
going to use your
credentials and your roles

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:04.940
within GCS in order to
get access to that data

00:32:04.940 --> 00:32:06.470
and to write new data.

00:32:06.470 --> 00:32:07.970
You enable rights
and data aging,

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:09.560
so we can treat that
as though it were

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:11.510
your data sitting within GCS.

00:32:11.510 --> 00:32:13.610
And you can do a test restore--

00:32:13.610 --> 00:32:15.000
highly recommended.

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.270
And then you can
continue using GCS

00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:19.380
for your incremental backups.

00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:25.310
So the first real quick
high-level best practices.

00:32:25.310 --> 00:32:29.000
From a deduplication standpoint,
of the things that we've

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:31.550
found with the
Cloud storage is we

00:32:31.550 --> 00:32:33.770
can use very large
deduplication sizes

00:32:33.770 --> 00:32:37.050
because we can do a range reads
within those blocks of data.

00:32:37.050 --> 00:32:40.663
So where we've seen
customers trip up,

00:32:40.663 --> 00:32:42.080
especially when
transferring data,

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:45.515
is on-prem they may be used
to writing a much smaller

00:32:45.515 --> 00:32:47.090
deduplication block size.

00:32:47.090 --> 00:32:49.220
However, when they
transfer that data

00:32:49.220 --> 00:32:52.630
as an auxiliary copy into
Cloud, it's not optimized.

00:32:52.630 --> 00:32:55.010
So then they have to do
a rehydration and then

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:57.360
a deduplication over.

00:32:57.360 --> 00:32:59.510
So the first time around
to Transfer Appliance,

00:32:59.510 --> 00:33:02.630
it's beneficial to just
have the right large block

00:33:02.630 --> 00:33:04.100
size for deduplication sets.

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:06.270
It's one of the best practices.

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:09.470
The other one is to
definitely disable

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:11.210
the backups and data aging.

00:33:11.210 --> 00:33:14.930
What that helps with is not
upsetting your SLAS on-prem.

00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:17.810
That way the system is
aware that that data

00:33:17.810 --> 00:33:20.580
is going to be inaccessible
for a period of time.

00:33:20.580 --> 00:33:24.150
And one of the benefits you
get is you don't have to,

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:28.160
with the platform,
have the fulls

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:30.590
sitting readable,
available in order for you

00:33:30.590 --> 00:33:32.780
to continue doing incrementals.

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:34.670
So obviously, from a
restore perspective,

00:33:34.670 --> 00:33:35.630
you've got to be aware of that.

00:33:35.630 --> 00:33:37.380
And by disabling it,
then you'll know what

00:33:37.380 --> 00:33:40.400
restores are not available.

00:33:40.400 --> 00:33:41.930
And then sufficient
bandwidth for

00:33:41.930 --> 00:33:43.640
interim and daily incrementals.

00:33:43.640 --> 00:33:46.970
And really, this is
the sizing question of,

00:33:46.970 --> 00:33:48.910
do I want to size
for peak, or do I

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:50.160
want to size for incrementals?

00:33:50.160 --> 00:33:52.940
So you still want to
consider your incrementals

00:33:52.940 --> 00:33:55.450
and start writing those
incrementals straight to GCS.

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:58.940
So what I really mean by that,
I illustrated on the right side.

00:33:58.940 --> 00:34:01.190
So for super simplification,
let's assume you've

00:34:01.190 --> 00:34:02.630
got 500 terabytes of data.

00:34:02.630 --> 00:34:07.670
You've got a gigabit bandwidth
available to do the transfer.

00:34:07.670 --> 00:34:09.739
And let's say you've
got about 2% change

00:34:09.739 --> 00:34:12.350
rate of net new data coming in.

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:14.330
One option is you
write the 500 terabytes

00:34:14.330 --> 00:34:16.070
as is through the
Transfer Appliance.

00:34:16.070 --> 00:34:17.778
Or if you were to do
it over the network,

00:34:17.778 --> 00:34:22.280
that would take about 51
days to transfer the data.

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:24.650
You deduplicate it
and compress it.

00:34:24.650 --> 00:34:27.989
That ends up being
about 100 terabytes,

00:34:27.989 --> 00:34:31.250
which you could write in
10 days over the network.

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:33.980
But then what you have to worry
about is, in those 10 days,

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:35.820
there's going to be
change rate generated,

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:37.940
which means then you have to
recursively catch up on that

00:34:37.940 --> 00:34:40.565
and recursively catch up on the
next and the next and the next.

00:34:40.565 --> 00:34:43.940
And, of course, we're
assuming there's no growth.

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:47.345
So the idea here is that that
100 terabytes deduplicated

00:34:47.345 --> 00:34:49.595
and compressed is written
into the Transfer Appliance.

00:34:49.595 --> 00:34:52.159
The Transfer Appliance to put
on the truck and sent across.

00:34:52.159 --> 00:34:55.250
Let's assume it takes two weeks
to get that data to show up

00:34:55.250 --> 00:34:58.290
in your GCS bucket.

00:34:58.290 --> 00:35:01.610
So in those 14 days, you've
generated another 20 terabytes

00:35:01.610 --> 00:35:02.310
worth of data.

00:35:02.310 --> 00:35:05.210
So now you're at the position
that you were earlier, which

00:35:05.210 --> 00:35:06.693
is, how do I transfer that?

00:35:06.693 --> 00:35:07.860
Do I do it over the network?

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:09.360
Do I get another
Transfer Appliance?

00:35:09.360 --> 00:35:12.770
So you don't want to do
either one, preferably.

00:35:12.770 --> 00:35:15.350
What you want to do is at the
bottom, where you're actually

00:35:15.350 --> 00:35:17.620
sending your incrementals--

00:35:17.620 --> 00:35:20.290
which is two terabytes
considering it's 2%--

00:35:20.290 --> 00:35:21.710
to GCS directly.

00:35:21.710 --> 00:35:23.920
So now we're talking
about, mathematically,

00:35:23.920 --> 00:35:26.140
about five hours to
transfer that data.

00:35:26.140 --> 00:35:27.993
So think of it as two buckets.

00:35:27.993 --> 00:35:29.410
You'll end up with
one bucket that

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:31.633
has your fulls or your
auxiliary copy data,

00:35:31.633 --> 00:35:33.550
and you'll have your
second bucket, which will

00:35:33.550 --> 00:35:34.900
have your incremental data.

00:35:34.900 --> 00:35:37.450
And from a platform
perspective, there's

00:35:37.450 --> 00:35:41.290
no complication, no
issue, for the system

00:35:41.290 --> 00:35:43.770
to be able to pick up
where the data lives.

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:45.520
So if you want to be
able to do a restore,

00:35:45.520 --> 00:35:47.200
it knows it to read
from bucket one,

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:50.080
and incremental bit
from your new bucket

00:35:50.080 --> 00:35:53.030
versus from your old bucket
in order to pull that.

00:35:53.030 --> 00:35:56.290
So the important aspect here
is that that 28 terabytes

00:35:56.290 --> 00:35:58.150
or three days, because
generating that data,

00:35:58.150 --> 00:36:01.953
while the appliance
is on the truck,

00:36:01.953 --> 00:36:03.370
and then you can
finally catch up.

00:36:03.370 --> 00:36:05.320
So the better approach
really is at the bottom

00:36:05.320 --> 00:36:07.278
where you're just sending
incrementals straight

00:36:07.278 --> 00:36:08.440
to GCS over your network.

00:36:08.440 --> 00:36:11.590
And what that end
up being is, yeah, I

00:36:11.590 --> 00:36:14.140
can keep my network
at a gigabit.

00:36:14.140 --> 00:36:17.647
I don't have to provision an
immense amount of bandwidth

00:36:17.647 --> 00:36:19.480
just to account for the
500 terabytes, which

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:20.802
would be a one-time transfer.

00:36:20.802 --> 00:36:23.260
And hopefully, you never have
to transfer the 500 terabytes

00:36:23.260 --> 00:36:26.590
back, so you never really
have to worry about that.

00:36:26.590 --> 00:36:27.090
Ben.

00:36:31.330 --> 00:36:32.860
BEN CHONG: Thanks, Deepak.

00:36:32.860 --> 00:36:35.590
All right, so just
a quick summary.

00:36:35.590 --> 00:36:38.400
So we have a Cloud Transfer
Service for cloud-to-cloud

00:36:38.400 --> 00:36:42.360
transfers, gsutil for
on-prem-to-cloud transfers

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:43.785
if you have enough bandwidth.

00:36:43.785 --> 00:36:45.910
And, obviously, if you
don't have enough bandwidth,

00:36:45.910 --> 00:36:47.040
there's Transfer Appliance.

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:49.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

